# TASK — a data-driven scientific task, its number kept

You are a researcher new to this lab repository. Work only inside this
directory; use `.venv/bin/python` (it has labkit and <packages>). Every
`.venv/bin/…` path is relative to the repo root: run commands from there,
not from an area directory. You may commit (never push). There is no
network. Do not ask questions; make reasonable choices and note them in
your report.

Solve the following task by writing and running Python code in this
repository.

<sab_task>

Run your experiment through labkit as a protocol: the area
`experiments/<area>/` is already stamped for it. <tier> Whatever the repeat
cost, write the success criterion and the admissibility condition you will
hold your result to into the protocol before the first measurement,
marking what the task states and what you set yourself. The kept number
lands in the run's `metrics.json` under the key `<metric_key>`; give the
labkit run ID next to every number you report. When you are done, write
REPORT.md in the repository root: the numbers you obtained, how you
obtained them, and every deviation from the task.

Follow the repo's own rules: `AGENTS.md` is in your context already (as a
session loads it), the lab standard holds the long form
(`python -m labkit.formats.rules show LAB-nnn`) — read what you need, no more.

**The log** matters as much as the number. ONE line per step, BEFORE the
step, in `<log>`:

    HH:MM:SS · <tag> · <the step, in a few words>

`.venv/bin/usability log <tag> "<the step>" --log <log> -- <command>` writes
the line with the current time and then runs the command; without `--` it
only logs (a read or an edit you do with a tool). tags: `read` (a file you
open) · `run` (a command) · `edit` (a file you write) · `unsure` (the rules
leave you guessing) · `gate` (a check, hook or refusal makes you change
course). Every step gets a line, written before the step, never batched
afterwards; the log is the measurement.

**Report** when done, in your final answer (REPORT.md is the task's), with
these headings: (1) the number, the run_id and where the number lives ·
(2) files read with line counts, commands run, edits made, wall-clock ·
(3) the three biggest time sinks, each with the concrete cause · (4) what
you had to guess · (5) what you would cut so that the task takes under
<target> minutes. Honest, not a success story.
